---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Demo from '../../../components/Demo.astro';
import HeadlessTailwindDemo from '../../../components/demos/HeadlessTailwindDemo.tsx';
import headlessTailwindSource from '../../../components/demos/HeadlessTailwindDemo.tsx?raw';
---

<DocsLayout title="Headless table with Tailwind | react-data-table-component" description="Build a sortable, filterable, selectable, paginated table with the headless hooks and Tailwind CSS utility classes, with no DataTable component and no library stylesheet.">
  <h1>Headless table with Tailwind</h1>

  <p>
    When your design system is Tailwind and you want to own every element, skip
    <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> and build the table from the <a href="/docs/headless">headless hooks</a>.
    The hooks hold sort, page, selection, and filter state. You write a plain <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> and
    style it with utility classes.
  </p>

  <p>
    If <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> with <a href="/docs/custom-styles">custom styles</a> or a
    <a href="/docs/themes">theme</a> gets you there, use that instead. It is far less code, and the header,
    checkboxes, and pagination come with their accessibility wiring.
  </p>

  <Demo
    title="Headless table with Tailwind"
    description="Sort by clicking a header, search and filter by status, select rows, and page through the results. Every element and class is in the demo source."
    code={headlessTailwindSource}
  >
    <HeadlessTailwindDemo client:visible={{ rootMargin: '200px' }} />
  </Demo>

  <h2>Import from the headless entry</h2>

  <CodeBlock lang="ts" code={`import {
  useColumns,
  useTableState,
  useTableData,
  useColumnFilter,
  SortOrder,
  type TableColumn,
} from 'react-data-table-component/headless';`} />

  <p>
    <code>react-data-table-component/headless</code> contains the hooks only. It does not load
    <code>&lt;DataTable /&gt;</code> or inject the library stylesheet, so nothing competes with your
    utility classes.
  </p>

  <h2>The pipeline</h2>

  <p>
    Four hooks, in this order. Filter the full sorted set first and slice the page last, so a search finds
    rows on every page.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`const { tableColumns, defaultSortColumn, defaultSortDirection } = useColumns(columns, {
  defaultSortFieldId: 'customer',
});

const { tableState, handleSort, handleChangePage, handleSelectedRow, handleSelectAllRows } =
  useTableState({ data, defaultSortColumn, defaultSortDirection, paginationPerPage: 5 });

const { selectedColumn, sortDirection, sortColumns, currentPage, rowsPerPage } = tableState;

const { sortedData } = useTableData({
  data, selectedColumn, sortDirection, sortColumns, currentPage, rowsPerPage,
});

const { filterValues, handleFilterChange, filteredData } = useColumnFilter(tableColumns);
const filteredRows = filteredData(sortedData);
const rows = filteredRows.slice((currentPage - 1) * rowsPerPage, currentPage * rowsPerPage);`} />

  <h2>Sortable headers</h2>

  <p>
    Put a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> inside each <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> and set <code>aria-sort</code> on
    the header cell. The button gives you keyboard support for free. Clicking the same column again
    cycles ascending, descending, then back to unsorted. Add <code>direction: SortOrder.DESC</code> (or
    <code>ASC</code>) to the action to force a direction instead.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`<th scope="col" aria-sort={ariaSort} className="px-3 py-2.5">
  <button
    type="button"
    onClick={() =>
      handleSort({
        type: 'SORT_CHANGE',
        selectedColumn: col,
        clearSelectedOnSort: false,
        additive: false,
        defaultSortDirection: SortOrder.ASC,
      })
    }
    className="inline-flex items-center gap-1 hover:text-gray-900 focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-blue-500"
  >
    {col.name}
  </button>
</th>`} />

  <h2>Row selection</h2>

  <p>
    Selection is driven by actions. For a row checkbox, pass the row and whether it is selected
    <em>before</em> the click. <code>rowCount</code> is the number of rows "select all" covers, which is how
    the hook knows when to set <code>tableState.allSelected</code>.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`const isSelected = (row: Invoice) => tableState.selectedRows.some(r => r.id === row.id);

<input
  type="checkbox"
  className="size-4 rounded border-gray-300 accent-blue-600"
  checked={isSelected(row)}
  onChange={() =>
    handleSelectedRow({
      type: 'SELECT_SINGLE_ROW',
      keyField: 'id',
      row,
      isSelected: isSelected(row),
      rowCount: filteredRows.length,
      singleSelect: false,
    })
  }
/>`} />

  <p>
    The header checkbox toggles between every filtered row and none. Set <code>indeterminate</code> through a
    ref, since React has no prop for it.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`<input
  type="checkbox"
  checked={tableState.allSelected}
  ref={el => {
    if (el) el.indeterminate = tableState.selectedRows.length > 0 && !tableState.allSelected;
  }}
  onChange={() =>
    handleSelectAllRows({
      type: 'SELECT_ALL_ROWS',
      keyField: 'id',
      rows: filteredRows,
      rowCount: filteredRows.length,
      mergeSelections: false,
    })
  }
/>`} />

  <h2>Filters reset the page and the selection</h2>

  <p>
    A filter changes how many pages there are and which rows "select all" covers. Go back to page 1 and
    clear the selection whenever one changes, so neither points at rows that are no longer shown.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="tsx" code={`function applyFilter(columnId: string, operator: 'contains' | 'equals', value: string) {
  handleFilterChange(columnId, { condition1: { operator, value } });
  handleClearSelectedRows();
  handleChangePage(1);
}

<input
  type="search"
  value={filterValues.customer?.condition1.value ?? ''}
  onChange={e => applyFilter('customer', 'contains', e.target.value)}
  className="rounded-md border border-gray-300 px-3 py-1.5 focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500/30"
/>`} />

  <h2>What you take on</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Markup and semantics: <code>&lt;table&gt;</code>, <code>scope</code>, <code>aria-sort</code>, and labels on every checkbox and input.</li>
    <li>Empty, loading, and error states.</li>
    <li>Responsive behavior, such as the <code>overflow-x-auto</code> wrapper in the demo.</li>
    <li>Dark mode, with Tailwind's <code>dark:</code> variants on the same elements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For keyboard grid navigation, column resizing, and pinning on your own markup, see the
    <code>useCellNavigation</code>, <code>useColumnResize</code>, and pinning helpers on the
    <a href="/docs/headless/reference">hook reference</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
